#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""profile-sync: copy the ChatGPT / OpenAI sign-in of a Chrome profile into a guardian browser profile, on request.

OPT-IN. Nothing runs this by itself: `apply` only installs the command (~/.local/bin/chatgpt-guardian-profile-sync, a
wrapper that passes this machine's module options to this file). The owner starts it by hand.

  profile-sync pull <home|glide> [--from DIR] [--expect-domain DOMAIN] [--dry-run]
  profile-sync status

Why: the guardian browsers (profiles `home` and `glide.vc` under ~/.local/share/agent-tools/chatgpt-guardian/profiles) are
signed in by hand, each on a private display, and a ChatGPT sign-in is lost whenever the session is. The dock Chrome (the
profile ~/.config/google-chrome, "Default") is where the owner signs in comfortably. Chrome refuses DevTools on its
default data directory, so the guardian cannot use that profile itself: `pull` moves the sign-in across instead.

`pull` copies ONLY the cookie rows of chatgpt.com and openai.com (the registrable domains, with every subdomain: chatgpt.com,
.chatgpt.com, auth.openai.com, .openai.com, ...) from the `Cookies` database of the SOURCE profile into the same database
of the guardian profile; the target's own rows of those domains are replaced, every other cookie is left alone. Local
Storage (a LevelDB, not copyable with the standard library) is NOT copied: ChatGPT's sign-in is the session cookie.
Rows are copied verbatim, `encrypted_value` included: both profiles belong to this user and the same Chrome binary, so the
key Chrome encrypts cookies with (keyring "Chrome Safe Storage", or its built-in fallback) is the same and a row decrypts
in either. No cookie value is ever printed, logged or put into an exit message: only counts per host and the encryption
prefix kind (v10 / v11 / plain).

`pull` in order:
  1. checks: the source Chrome is CLOSED (no process runs with --user-data-dir=<source>, and the SingletonLock of the
     source is not owned by a live process), both Cookies databases exist, the source holds ChatGPT/OpenAI rows;
     --dry-run stops here and prints the plan (no unit is stopped, nothing is written);
  2. takes the per-profile lock that `relogin run` takes too (one of them at a time, through verification and rollback;
     ~/.local/state/ubuntu-tweaks/chatgpt-guardian-locks/<profile>.lock), stops the target's guardian
     units (guardian, then observer, then browser: relogin's order and code), waits until no process holds the target
     profile, checks again that the source is closed;
  3. backs up the target Cookies database through the sqlite3 backup API into
     ~/.local/state/ubuntu-tweaks/chatgpt-guardian-profile-sync/<workspace>/ (mode 0600, the newest three are kept,
     pruned after every completed pull or rollback, never after a failed restore);
     the source is read through the same API into memory, never a raw file copy of a database;
  4. replaces the target rows in ONE transaction (all or nothing), then integrity-checks the database;
  5. starts the BROWSER ALONE (after `systemctl reset-failed` on the three units: the browser units allow two starts per
     five minutes and the verification and a rollback each need one) and asks it through DevTools which account is signed in (relogin's await_account: the
     semantics of `relogin check --live`, the browser's answer only); the observer and the guardian are started only
     after that is confirmed, because a guardian that meets rejected cookies (a 401) latches a human-challenge stop that
     restoring the cookies does not clear. A browser that restarted without a chatgpt.com tab gets a temporary one
     (DevTools /json/new) for the question and leaves it open (the glide guardian's start check needs one too). Rules:
       glide: the email domain must be the glide account's (module option glide_email_domain, e.g. app.glide.vc; the
              guardian unit starts only for it, so --expect-domain may only repeat it; the relogin marker is written on
              success like after `relogin run`);
       home:  a VISIBLE email domain, and it must NOT be the glide account's (a mix-up guard); --expect-domain DOMAIN
              requires exactly that domain instead;
     then the three units are waited for with the guardian's health line (relogin's wait_stack);
  6. when step 5 does not confirm it, or the command is interrupted from step 2 on, the backup is restored (stop all
     three units, restore, start all three: relogin's order) and the command fails; signals are ignored from then on.
     When the RESTORE fails (exit 5) no unit is started again: the unconfirmed cookies are still in place. Every unit is
     tried when stopping for the restore.
Every database copy is bounded (60 s, CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_DB_TIMEOUT): a database another process holds locked ends
the command instead of hanging. SQLite's own error text can quote a cookie value, so only the error class is printed.

The sign-in the dock Chrome holds is ONE ChatGPT account at a time, so the workflow is: sign in to the wanted account in
the dock Chrome, CLOSE that Chrome, run `pull home` (or `pull glide`). For the other workspace sign in to the other
account, close, pull again.

`status` is read-only: for each guardian profile it asks the running browser through its DevTools port which ChatGPT
account domain is signed in (nothing else leaves the browser), and reports whether the source Chrome is open and how many
ChatGPT/OpenAI cookie rows its profile holds (counted only while that Chrome is closed). It always exits 0 unless the
usage or configuration is wrong.

Limits (honest ones): this moves LIVE SESSION COOKIES between profiles of the same user. A session that ChatGPT or Cloudflare
bound to the device or the browser instance may be rejected in the copy; ChatGPT may sign out the other copy of a session
(so the dock Chrome can lose its sign-in after the guardian refreshed the token, or the reverse); the backup holds the target's
previous cookies (secrets at rest, 0600, the same protection as the profile itself).

Environment (all optional; tests and experiments set them so the real profiles are never touched):
  CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_SOURCE    source user-data-dir   default ~/.config/google-chrome (--from wins)
  CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_TARGET    target profile dir     default ~/.local/share/agent-tools/chatgpt-guardian/profiles/<home|glide.vc>
  CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_PORT      DevTools port          default: module option home_port / glide_port (9226 / 9223)
  CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_BACKUPS   backup directory       default ~/.local/state/ubuntu-tweaks/chatgpt-guardian-profile-sync
  CHATGPT_PROFILE_SYNC_DB_TIMEOUT seconds one database copy may wait for a lock   default 60
  and relogin's own CHATGPT_RELOGIN_SYSTEMCTL / _JOURNALCTL / _STOP_TIMEOUT / _VERIFY_TIMEOUT / _ACCOUNT_TIMEOUT /
  _PROBE_TIMEOUT / _POLL / _DOMAIN (see relogin); the unit names, profile, port and marker are derived per workspace here.
Module options come as JSON in UBUNTU_TWEAK_MODULE_CONFIG (the installed wrapper sets it); the language in
UBUNTU_TWEAK_LANG (ru or en).
Exit codes: 0 ok; 1 failed, the target is unchanged or was restored from the backup; 2 usage or configuration; 3 refused,
a Chrome still has the source or the target profile open, or `relogin run` / another pull holds that profile's lock (nothing changed); 4 the copy was applied but the sign-in was not
confirmed, the backup is restored; 5 the restore failed, no unit was started again and the backup path is printed (restore it by hand); 130 interrupted.
"""

